          body: "Jesus’ words are not a wrong medical prognosis — Lazarus really does die (v. 14). He means that death will not be the *end* of the story: this illness is *aimed at* God’s glory, and Lazarus’ tomb will become the stage for it. And here is the note that unsettles every easy reading: because he loved this family (v. 5), **he stayed two days longer** where he was (v. 6). His delay is deliberate, not indifferent — he lets Lazarus die so that what he does will be unmistakably resurrection, not recovery. Love that looks like absence, a silence with a purpose inside it: John sets this puzzle at the head of the chapter so that Martha’s and Mary’s identical reproach — *“Lord, if you had been here…”* (vv. 21, 32) — becomes the question the whole story answers.",
