      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a11.33.1',
          kind: "grammar",
          refs: ['g11.33.13', 'g11.33.17', 'g11.38.4'],
          lemma: "ἐνεβριμήσατο τῷ πνεύματι",
          title: "“Deeply moved” — the shudder of a verb",
          body: "The Greek *enebrimēsato* is startling. Elsewhere it describes a *snort* of anger, the sound of a warhorse, a stern rebuke (Mark 1:43); it is not gentle sympathy but something fierce. Some read it as Jesus’ *indignation* — at death itself, that obscene intruder in his Father’s world, and at the unbelief and wailing around the grave. Others hear the sheer force of his grief, emotion so strong it convulses him; John adds that he **“troubled himself”** (the same word used of his own dread in 12:27), as if he took the turmoil up deliberately. Both are true to the scene. Here is God-in-flesh at a tomb, and he does not stand above the horror in serene detachment — he trembles with anger and sorrow at what death has done, *even though he is seconds from undoing it.* He will raise Lazarus; he weeps first. The power that conquers death is not indifferent to death’s wreckage — it feels every inch of it.",
        },
      ],
