      annotations: [
        {
          id: 'a11.43.1',
          kind: "concept",
          refs: ['g11.43.6', 'g11.43.7', 'g11.43.8'],
          lemma: "φωνῇ μεγάλῃ … Λάζαρε, δεῦρο ἔξω",
          title: "“Lazarus, come out!”",
          body: "The whole Gospel has been building toward a voice. Jesus said the hour was coming when *“the dead will hear the voice of the Son of God, and those who hear will live”* (5:25), and here, in a single loud command, that voice reaches into a tomb. The old preachers loved to say he called Lazarus by *name* — had he not, every grave in the district might have emptied. The command is sheer creative word: like *“Let there be light,”* it makes what it says. Death has no answer to it. Note the pattern that will echo at the Gospel’s end: a stone rolled away, grave-clothes, a man alive who was dead. But there is a decisive difference — Lazarus comes out still **bound**, hand and foot, needing others to unwrap him, and he will die again. He is a sign, not the substance. When Jesus rises, the cloths will lie folded and empty (20:6–7), and death will be behind him forever. Lazarus is given back his old life; Christ breaks through into a new one.",
        },
      ],
